export type IsPairedFn = (input: string) => boolean;

## Intent
Exercise "matching-brackets": implement `isPaired`. Given a string containing brackets `[]`, braces `{}`, parentheses `()`, or any combination thereof, verify that any and all pairs are matched and nested correctly. Any other characters should be ignored. For example, `"{what is (42)}?"` is balanced and `"[text}"` is not.

## Examples (deep equality)
{"args":["[]"],"expected":true,"name":"Matching Brackets > paired square brackets"}
{"args":[""],"expected":true,"name":"Matching Brackets > empty string"}
{"args":["[["],"expected":false,"name":"Matching Brackets > unpaired brackets"}

## Rules
No new imports.
No effects: never use Date.now(), new Date() without arguments, performance, Math.random, crypto, console, process, globalThis, eval, Function, fetch, require, import(), setTimeout or setInterval.



Reply with JSON only: {"body": "<statements>", "imports": []}. Add {"from": "<specifier>", "names": ["<name>"]} to "imports" only for a new import the Rules allow.
